#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""oc-census — READ-ONLY pending-branch census (runbook Step 7 step 0).

WHAT IT ANSWERS
  For every local branch b: how many of its commits are NOT already in the base
  (`git cherry <base> <b>` reports `+`), and of those, how many belong to the
  branch TIP's own lane (its `Session-Id` trailer), carry NO trailer, or carry a
  DIFFERENT lane's trailer.  Those three arms PARTITION the first:

      plus_total == plus_own + plus_untrailered + plus_other

  The identity is ENFORCED, not merely printed: a row that fails to add up is an
  internal error (rc 3), never a silently-wrong number.

WHY (runbook Step 7 step 0, v0.4.170 + v0.4.214)
  The runbook sanctions exactly ONE pending signal:

      pending_lane(b) = commits `git cherry <newbase> <b>` reports as `+`,
                        whose Session-Id trailer names the BRANCH TIP's lane

  A non-zero AGGREGATE `+` is not proof of pending work: patch-id matching is
  blind to a change already present by another route (v0.4.214 — 10 of 10
  lane-scoped `+` markers on two lanes were content-present).  So the census
  SPLITS the aggregate into its three arms AND runs a CONTENT leg over every
  `+` instead of trusting either, and it never prints a cut sha — it reports
  the counts and the base the branch sits on.

CLASSIFICATION (spec §4 — the wording rule must survive into the OUTPUT)
  A lane-scoped 0 NEVER means "the branch is absorbed": it means none of the
  UNABSORBED commits carry that lane's Session-Id.  Every row therefore carries
  a `class`, worded so a reader cannot invert it:

    ALREADY_ABSORBED   the tip is reachable from the base AND lane-scoped
                       pending is 0 -> nothing here is pending for ANYONE
                       (a terminal state, so it is tested FIRST)
    UNATTRIBUTABLE     no Session-Id on the tip; may or may not be yours
                       -> verify by CONTENT
    MALFORMED_TRAILER  the tip's Session-Id is not a UUID (the real `7198` on
                       feat/notify-on-roll) -> its own class, NEVER a lane, so
                       it cannot inflate the rollup's lane count
    NOT_YOURS          (--lane) the tip belongs to another lane; its lane-scoped
                       commits are THAT lane's, not yours
    YOUR_PENDING_ZERO  (--lane) your tip is already in the base by patch-id
                       match -- the AGGREGATE counted other lanes' and
                       untrailered commits
    YOUR_PENDING       (--lane) corrected DOWNWARD from the aggregate
    LANE_PENDING / LANE_ZERO_OWN   the same two facts with no asker given
    ERROR              the cherry leg failed for this branch (plus_total -1)

  LANE KEY = the branch TIP's `Session-Id` trailer.  NEVER the issue number
  embedded in the branch NAME, and never a roster uuid column: both were proven
  wrong on 2026-09-19 (spec §4).

  ROLLUP  `meta.rollup` sums `plus_own` per lane key, keyed on the tip_lane
  values actually OBSERVED (a lane that tips no branch is absent, not a phantom
  zero), and holds non-UUID keys in a SEPARATE map so a garbage trailer can
  never be counted as a lane.  A lane whose own total is 0 across every branch
  it tips is exactly the population an AGGREGATE-based advisory names falsely
  (`zero_own`): the tool reports that population -- it cannot know what a given
  advisory actually said, and does not pretend to.

READ-ONLY
  Only `rev-parse` / `cherry` / `for-each-ref` / `log --format` / `merge-base`,
  plus the content leg's two reads: `ls-tree -r -z` (the base tree) and
  `diff-tree --stdin --raw` (each pending commit's post-image blobs).
  No ref is written, no store is touched, no cut is computed.

NAMING COLLISION (spec §7) — `%oc-census%`, not `%census%`
  `oc-census` is substring-adjacent to the EXISTING `oc-harvest-census` (the
  upstream-PR harvest registry, ~100 KB, 30+ fork issues).  `grep -i census` and
  SQL `LIKE '%census%'` therefore hit BOTH.  Measured 2026-09-19: `%census%`
  returned 200+ rows, every one of them the OTHER tool; `%oc-census%` returned a
  clean set.  The name is KEPT (design H ruling) — the mitigation is this
  predicate, not a rename.

COST (measured 2026-09-20, 383 branches, base origin/main)
  `git cherry <base> <b>` costs ~1.4 s/branch: the expense is the LEFT side
  (base commits not in b), whose patch-ids are recomputed per branch.  Serial
  sweep = ~520 s; a bounded worker pool = ~150 s.  `git cherry <merge-base> <b>`
  is 20x faster but DEGENERATES to the whole-branch aggregate (plus == right-side
  count) — the exact wrong answer this tool exists to correct — so it is not
  used.  Branches whose tip is already reachable from the base skip `cherry`
  entirely (their plus is 0 by construction; one `for-each-ref --merged` call
  settles all of them at once).

  MEMORY: the sweep is a pool of SUBPROCESSES (w<=4 default), never threads
  holding bulk data; peak RSS ~17 MB on the 383-branch sweep.  The ops cgroup
  caps at MemoryHigh=768M, so no memory-hungry design is admissible here.

OUTPUT
  Human table by default; `--json` emits an OBJECT carrying the ref-state
  (repo, base, base_sha) BESIDE `rows`, because a census figure without the
  ref-state it was taken at is unreproducible (spec §2: the census is a
  point-in-time measurement, never a constant).

EXIT: 0 census produced · 1 refused (repo or base unresolvable) ·
      2 usage · 3 invariant break (internal, should be impossible).
"""

